        ":question/problem": "Use the substitution $u=x^{2} + 1$. Which integral is obtained?\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}} x e^{x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=x^{2} + 1$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(2 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nTransform both bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0&\\Longrightarrow u=1\\\\x=\\sqrt{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}&\\Longrightarrow u=\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}} x e^{x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int_{1}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 1} e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformed integral is\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int_{1}^{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + 1} e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\n$$",
